# Can an Assassin death-attack a target that is NOT relaxed?
#
# bd inc-tek.24, finding PA-03-F28 of bd inc-tek.8.3.
#
# THE DEFECT. The class page attaches the out-of-combat condition to the
# assassin alone (lib/prestige.irh:520-524). The gate demanded it of the target
# too:  if (EActor->isFlatFooted() && EVictim->isFlatFooted()).
# isFlatFooted() is not the D&D condition; it is a per-creature out-of-combat
# counter, FFCount > min(5,10+Mod(A_WIS)) (inc/Creature.h:567-568). Any strike
# zeroes it for both fighters (src/Fight.cpp:3150-3151), so a target already
# fighting -- one the party was holding, say -- could never be assassinated.
#
# WHAT THIS RUN ARRANGES, and why it is not the party fight it stands for.
# The run needs a victim whose FFCount is low while the assassin's is high.
# Staging a real melee between a companion and the victim would work and would
# also make the measurement depend on monster AI timing. The engine sets the
# same field itself, deterministically, at creation: Monster::Initialize gives
# a creature with Improved Initiative FFCount 0 and everything else FFCount 20
# (src/Monster.cpp:1443-1446). So the run summons one of each and strikes both.
# The only difference between the two victims is the one field the fix reads.
#
#   goblin      no Improved Initiative -> FFCount 20 -> relaxed
#   grippli     Improved Initiative    -> FFCount 0  -> not relaxed
#               (lib/mon2.irh:3865)
#
# THE ORACLE is the ability's own message. Death Attack announces itself on
# either outcome -- "You assassinate the <victim>!" when the Fortitude save
# fails and "The <victim> resists your death attack!" when it holds
# (lib/prestige.irh:677-682). Either line proves the gate opened; neither can
# appear if it did not. So the check does not depend on a saving throw.
#
# THE TWO STATES, and why the control is not optional:
#   goblin      the message must appear BEFORE the fix and after it. This is
#               what stops a "fix" that merely broke the ability from passing.
#   grippli     the message appears ONLY after the fix.
#
# THE OTHER CONDITIONS ARE HELD FAVOURABLE, and one line proves it. Death
# Attack also requires that the target not perceive the assassin, so both
# victims are put to sleep first: a sleeping creature perceives nothing
# (src/Vision.cpp:416-419) and the engine skips the Move Silently against
# Listen contest for it (src/Fight.cpp:3186-3196). That contest is a d20 roll
# and it DID decide an earlier draft of this file -- "The goblin hears you!" --
# which is exactly the kind of dependency a regression check must not carry.
#
# Sleeping is not enough on its own, because the sleeper wakes as the blow
# lands. The engine's own report that the blow arrived unperceived is the line
# "Catching him unaware" (src/Fight.cpp:3155-3157), and the check REQUIRES it
# on both strikes. It separates the two failures that otherwise look the same:
# a victim that noticed him, and a gate that refused a victim he approached
# perfectly.
#
# THE WAIT BETWEEN THE TWO STRIKES. The assassin's own FFCount is zeroed by his
# own blow, so after the first strike he is no longer relaxed and NOTHING can
# be measured until it recovers. Ten searches pass more than the six turns the
# threshold needs. The second victim is summoned after the wait, not before, so
# that its own counter is still fresh when it is struck.
#
# Freeze Monsters is on so that nothing retaliates during the wait: a blow
# landing on the assassin would zero his counter again and quietly void the
# second measurement.
#
# tools/check_death_attack.sh reads the two dumps. SEED matters: the attribute
# rolls and the offered feats move with it.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Your options will place the game in Explore Mode. Continue? -- yes

@choose "Elf"
@choose "Elf (Standard)"
@choose "Rogue"
a                       # Attributes: the first of the offered sets
@choose "Neutral"       # the class refuses a Good alignment
@choose "Alertness"
@until "Unspent Skill Ranks" a          # however many feats are asked for

# The four ranks the Assassin's gate reads, then the rest, because character
# generation will not release a character with a rank unspent.
@cursorto:mark "Disguise" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Poison Use" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Move Silently" UP
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Hide in Shadows" UP
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Bluff" UP
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Alchemy" UP
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Appraise" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Balance" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Climb" DOWN
RIGHT*3
ENTER y # Done, and confirm keeping the ranks

a       # Personality archetype

ESC     # dismiss the arrival-room description; now in play
w       # wizard mode
y       # "Enter wizard mode?" -- yes
w       # [w] Wizard Mode Switches
# The list draws no cursor a screen dump can see, so this counts rows against
# the option table (src/Tables.cpp:2610-2656). The cursor opens on the first
# row and SPACE does not move it.
DOWN*2
SPACE   # 3  Free Advancement:  NO -> YES
DOWN*8
SPACE   # 11 Freeze Monsters:   NO -> YES
ESC

d                               # character sheet
@until "Class  Rogue 5" G       # five core levels, for the ranks they carry

# The gate wants Disguise 10, Poison Use 10, Hide 7 and Move Silently 7.
S
@expect "Unspent Skill Ranks"
@cursorto:mark "Disguise" DOWN
RIGHT*8
@cursorto:mark "Poison Use" DOWN
RIGHT*8
@cursorto:mark "Move Silently" UP
RIGHT*8
@cursorto:mark "Hide in Shadows" UP
RIGHT*8
ENTER y

P                               # [P]restige Classes
@expect "Choose a class:"
@choose "Assassin"
@expect "Assassin 0"            # the gate let him in

@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Assassin"
@while "Choose a feat" a

# The Assassin's 1st level hands over a spellbook (lib/prestige.irh:597-607).
# It lands in the In-the-Air slot and the game will not leave inventory mode
# while anything is there, so it must be stowed before any further keystroke.
ESC
@while "Air':?" s
@while "ESC:Done" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d

@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Assassin"
@while "Choose a feat" a
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Assassin"
@while "Choose a feat" a
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Assassin"
@while "Choose a feat" a
@expect "Assassin 4"            # caster level 4, enough for the 1st-level sleep
ESC
@until "HP:" ESC

# Wizard mode teaches him Sleep. Knowing a spell is not enough to cast it --
# arcane casting wants the spellbook in hand -- and the Assassin's own
# spellbook does not carry it.
w
@choose "Learn Any Spell"
"Sleep" ENTER
@until "HP:" ESC

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- control
# A goblin has no Improved Initiative, so Monster::Initialize leaves it at
# FFCount 20 and it counts as relaxed. The old gate and the new one agree here.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Goblinkin"
@choose "goblin"
RIGHT ENTER
@until "HP:" ESC
@dump:0001-goblin-summoned

m
@cursorto:mark "Sleep" DOWN
ENTER
RIGHT ENTER
@until "HP:" ESC
@dump:0002-goblin-asleep

RIGHT                           # walk into it: the attack
@while "Confirm attack" y
@until "HP:" ESC
@dump:0003-goblin-struck

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the wait
# The blow above zeroed the assassin's own counter. Ten searches carry it back
# over the threshold of five.
s s s s s s s s s s
@until "HP:" ESC
@dump:0004-rested

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the test
# A grippli has Improved Initiative, so it is created at FFCount 0 and is not
# relaxed. Before the fix this is the case the gate refused. It is also the
# easiest of the Improved Initiative roster to hit (Def 13), which matters: a
# miss still zeroes the assassin's own counter and would waste the strike.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Reptiles"
@choose "grippli"
LEFT ENTER
@until "HP:" ESC
@dump:0005-grippli-summoned

m
@cursorto:mark "Sleep" DOWN
ENTER
LEFT ENTER
@until "HP:" ESC
@dump:0006-grippli-asleep

LEFT                            # walk into it: the attack
@while "Confirm attack" y
@until "HP:" ESC
@dump:0007-grippli-struck

@quit
